    "seo": {
      "title": "Asiatech: Data Centers, Colocation and Cloud Services | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Asiatech's advertised infrastructure role, its connection to Cloud.ir, and the distinction between server colocation and cloud services."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connecting facilities with digital services",
        "content": "Asiatech's infrastructure role is visible in two complementary kinds of public material. Its self-described official communications channel advertises dedicated servers, cloud servers, dedicated bandwidth, and colocation. Cloud.ir's company profile separately states that its infrastructure runs on Asiatech data centers. Together, these sources describe both physical hosting and services delivered above that physical layer. [s1][s2]\n\nThis relationship is useful for understanding Iran's cloud market. A data center supplies the environment in which equipment operates; a cloud service supplies a way to obtain and manage computing resources. The two can be closely connected without being interchangeable. Cloud.ir's website attributes its rights to the company named Asr Dadehaye Asiatech, but the inspected page alone does not establish every legal or ownership relationship within the wider Asiatech business. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "colocation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Colocation starts with the customer's equipment",
        "content": "Colocation means placing customer-owned servers in a provider's hosting environment. Asiatech's channel explicitly presents the service for businesses that have already bought servers and need data-center space and network access. Its descriptions mention environmental temperature and humidity management, fire detection and suppression, monitoring, and support. [s1]\n\nThese are advertised service features, rather than published inspection results. The distinction matters because buying space for an existing machine leaves a different set of responsibilities from renting a virtual server. The channel also lists dedicated-server and cloud-server services separately, indicating that customers are being offered different infrastructure arrangements. The inspected posts do not provide a complete contractual allocation of hardware, operating-system, or application responsibilities. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "cloud",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the Cloud.ir connection",
        "content": "Cloud.ir's catalogue lists cloud servers, cloud data centers, cloud switches, domain management, content delivery, and cloud storage. These names indicate a portfolio covering computing, networking, and data delivery. They should not be treated as proof that every product is available in every facility or under identical service terms. [s3]\n\nThe provider's profile says that its cloud activity began in Solar Hijri year 1399. That statement concerns Cloud.ir's service history; it should not be substituted for an Asiatech incorporation date. The same profile places Cloud.ir infrastructure on Asiatech facilities and makes broad claims about standards and reliability. Those claims require facility-specific documents before they can support a certification statement about a named site. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpretation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the evidence establishes",
        "content": "The documented connection between a cloud platform and an underlying facility helps explain the company's relevance beyond website hosting. It shows how a service catalogue can depend on a separate physical infrastructure layer. NIST's cloud definition provides a useful general distinction: cloud computing concerns on-demand access to a shared pool of configurable resources, rather than merely the presence of servers in a building. [s2][s4]\n\nThe strongest supported conclusion is therefore about Asiatech's advertised role in an infrastructure ecosystem. The inspected sources do not establish its national market share, independently measured service quality, or the capacity of individual sites. A fuller company account needs dated corporate disclosures and facility evidence, especially to describe the business precisely at the requested research cutoff.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      }
