    "description": "Data center services provide the physical environment and supporting infrastructure in which servers operate. In Iran, provider descriptions include colocation, server rental, and cloud-related services, but these arrangements assign different roles to the customer and the operator.",
    "tags": ["Data centers", "Colocation", "Infrastructure", "Reliability", "Cloud computing"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T19:11:39Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T19:11:39Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Data Center Services in Iran: Facilities, Colocation and Cloud | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An introduction to Iranian data center services, customer-owned server colocation, cloud infrastructure, and the meaning and limits of Tier certification."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The physical foundation of online services",
        "content": "A data center is part of the physical foundation beneath online services. Power, cooling, and equipment distribution paths affect whether computing equipment can continue operating during maintenance or failures. Uptime Institute's infrastructure classifications focus on these capabilities, while its operational sustainability framework addresses the management practices that also influence outcomes. A building's equipment specification is therefore only part of the reliability question. [s1]\n\nIn the Iranian market, the phrase 'data center services' can cover several commercial arrangements. Asiatech's public channel distinguishes customer-owned server colocation from dedicated and cloud servers. ParsPack's enterprise page separately lists data-center space and database services. These examples establish a range of advertised offerings, rather than a complete inventory of Iranian facilities or their capacity. [s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "service-models",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Space, servers, and cloud resources",
        "content": "Colocation begins with equipment the customer already owns. The provider supplies an environment in which to place it, together with contracted supporting services. Asiatech describes this arrangement for businesses that have purchased servers but need hosting space and network access; its promotional posts also mention environmental controls and fire protection. Those descriptions explain the service model without proving that a particular facility meets a particular performance threshold. [s3]\n\nCloud computing describes a different layer. NIST defines it around on-demand network access to pooled, configurable resources that can be provisioned and released with limited management effort. Renting rack space alone does not establish those cloud characteristics. A facility may host a cloud platform, customer equipment, or both, but its physical infrastructure and the customer's service interface remain distinct concepts. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reliability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a Tier claim can establish",
        "content": "Uptime Institute distinguishes basic infrastructure, redundant capacity, concurrent maintainability, and fault tolerance within its Tier system. Concurrent maintainability concerns planned removal of components or distribution paths without interrupting operations. Fault tolerance adds protection against an individual equipment failure or distribution-path interruption. These are infrastructure capabilities, not interchangeable descriptions of application performance. [s1][s2]\n\nCertification is a separate evidentiary step: Uptime Institute describes it as verification that the relevant standard has been applied. Its certification process distinguishes design documents, constructed facilities, and operational sustainability. An article should consequently identify the facility and certification scope before assigning a certified status. A provider's general reference to standards cannot establish all of those details. Nor does a facility classification, by itself, supply an annual record of customer-visible uptime. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "layers",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "From facilities to application services",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Facility power and cooling\"] -->|\"Support equipment operation\"| B[\"Physical servers and networks\"]\n  B -->|\"Host customer-owned equipment\"| C[\"Colocation arrangement\"]\n  B -->|\"Supply underlying resources\"| D[\"Cloud resource pool\"]\n  D -->|\"Provides configurable resources\"| E[\"Customer cloud workloads\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This conceptual diagram separates physical support from service delivery. It does not describe a particular Iranian facility or imply that every colocation operator also offers cloud computing.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iranian-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading Iranian service descriptions carefully",
        "content": "The inspected Iranian examples reveal different levels of detail. Asiatech's channel explains the intended colocation customer and supporting features. ParsPack's enterprise page identifies data-center space as an offering, but provides little facility-specific technical evidence in that section. It also lists database clustering and replication, illustrating how operational services can appear beside physical hosting in the same enterprise catalogue. [s3][s4]\n\nFor readers exploring the sector, the useful distinction is between what is being sold and what has been demonstrated. A catalogue establishes an offering; a facility document establishes a specified design or certification scope; an operational record establishes measured experience. The available evidence supports this explanation of service models, but a comprehensive account of Iran's regional capacity and operating performance still requires further research. [s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
